(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-premalignant-and-malignant-lesions-vignettes"]={"title":"Pre-Malignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 66-year-old man with fair skin has a small red, conical, hard nodule on his lower lip that has ulcerated over two months. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Keratoacanthoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Basal cell carcinoma","Actinic keratosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and the lip is one of the named high-risk sites. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 57-year-old has had itchy scaly plaques on the trunk for four years, several larger than 5 cm. They have been treated as eczema and then as psoriasis without lasting response, and he has patchy hair loss within some plaques. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma","Psoriasis","Eczematous dermatitis","Tinea corporis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — chronicity, treatment resistance and follicular involvement together. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has an ulcer on his ear that has not healed in three months. The base is firm and indurated. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Basal cell carcinoma","Actinic keratosis","Kaposi sarcoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A non-healing ulcer with an indurated base is one of the presentations the deck lists, and induration is on its red-flag list. The ear is also one of its named high-risk sites — with mucosal surfaces, lip, scalp, temple, nose and genitalia — all of which carry higher recurrence and metastatic risk. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man newly diagnosed with human immunodeficiency virus has several red-purple plaques on his legs. Which examination does the deck call essential, and what might you find?","choices":["Scalp examination — follicular lesions are common and may be the presenting site","Oral examination — hard palate lesions are common and may be the presenting site","Nail examination — subungual lesions are common and may be the presenting site","Ocular examination — eyelid lesions are common and may be the presenting site"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the mouth may declare the disease before anywhere else. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man has an ivory-white, scar-like plaque on his cheek with no injury to account for it. On careful inspection the pink edge appears to extend further than the visible lesion. Which subtype is this?","choices":["Morpheaform basal cell carcinoma","Superficial basal cell carcinoma","Nodular basal cell carcinoma","Pigmented basal cell carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A scar-like, ivory-white plaque with no injury to explain it is the morpheaform (sclerosing) subtype, and the edge running past the visible lesion is the concern: it spreads subclinically beyond what you can see, which is why it is an indication for Mohs micrographic surgery. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Two patients present the same morning. One has had intense blistering sunburns on holidays but works indoors; the other has worked outdoors for forty years with steady daily exposure. Which pattern favours which diagnosis?","choices":["Both patterns favour melanoma, since keratinocyte carcinoma is not ultraviolet-driven","Intense intermittent exposure favours basal cell carcinoma; prolonged cumulative exposure favours squamous cell carcinoma","Intense intermittent exposure favours squamous cell carcinoma; prolonged cumulative exposure favours basal cell carcinoma","Both patterns favour basal cell carcinoma equally, since ultraviolet exposure is not subdivided in this way"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the exposure pattern is a genuine discriminator. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 70-year-old has a biopsy-proven invasive squamous cell carcinoma 1.4 cm across on the temple. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Programmed death 1 blockade, since a lesion over 1 cm is by definition advanced disease","Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy, since the temple tolerates destruction well cosmetically","Mohs micrographic surgery, since the temple is a high-risk site and the lesion exceeds 1 cm on the face","Imiquimod or topical fluorouracil, since topical therapy is appropriate for invasive disease at this size"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — two separate Mohs indications are met. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"You have just cleared a patient's actinic keratoses with a course of field therapy. She asks whether that is the end of it. What is the most appropriate response?","choices":["A repeat course every six months will prevent any recurrence","Treatment reduces the lesion burden but the surrounding field stays at risk, so surveillance continues","Clearance means the surrounding field is no longer at risk and routine follow-up can stop","She should return only if a lesion becomes painful or bleeds"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — ongoing surveillance remains necessary despite successful therapy. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 61-year-old's melanoma measures 0.9 mm Breslow thickness, but her report notes ulceration and a high mitotic rate. Which is the most appropriate interpretation?","choices":["Sentinel lymph node biopsy should be deferred until the lesion recurs locally after re-excision","Sentinel lymph node biopsy should be offered or discussed, because additional histologic risk factors lower the threshold to 0.8 mm","Sentinel lymph node biopsy is not indicated, because the threshold is a fixed 1.0 mm regardless of other features","Sentinel lymph node biopsy is mandatory, because ulceration alone converts this to advanced disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — ulceration, high mitotic rate and lymphovascular invasion are the three named factors. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"You suspect a basal cell carcinoma on a patient's shoulder. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic step, and why does it matter which subtype comes back?","choices":["Shave or punch biopsy, because the CLINICAL subtype determines behaviour and dictates treatment selection","Wide local excision with 1 cm margins, because tissue diagnosis and treatment are achieved together","Sentinel lymph node biopsy, because nodal status determines treatment selection","Shave or punch biopsy, because the HISTOLOGIC subtype determines behaviour and dictates treatment selection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — histology, not clinical appearance, drives the plan. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A patient with cutaneous T-cell lymphoma develops enlarged inguinal nodes. What does the deck say these may represent, and what is required?","choices":["They may be benign dermatopathic change or lymphoma involvement, and require directed biopsy or imaging to distinguish","They always indicate systemic lymphoma involvement and warrant immediate systemic chemotherapy without any directed biopsy or imaging","They are always benign dermatopathic change and need no directed biopsy, imaging or further assessment","They indicate regional infection rather than lymphoma and warrant a course of oral antibiotics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — enlargement alone does not establish spread. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has a nail unit squamous cell carcinoma confirmed on biopsy. Which surgical approach does the deck prefer, and when is amputation considered?","choices":["Topical fluorouracil to the nail fold and bed; amputation is never indicated at any depth","Complete margin-controlled surgery — Mohs or wide surgical excision","Partial or limited destructive treatment such as curettage or cryotherapy; amputation is reserved for bone invasion","Digital amputation as the automatic first treatment for any nail unit carcinoma, whatever the depth or extent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — amputation is reserved for bone invasion or disease that cannot otherwise be cleared, and partial or limited destructive treatment carries a higher recurrence risk. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A patient presents with a lesion you have not seen before. Before you commit to a diagnosis, what does the deck ask you to do?","choices":["Characterise it systematically","Name the most likely diagnosis, then look only for the features that support it","Photograph it and wait for the dermatology opinion before describing anything","Biopsy immediately, since description adds nothing once tissue is available"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — describe before naming. Lesion type, colour, texture, border, size, distribution, palpability, ulceration, bleeding, induration and evolution. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 58-year-old patient asks you to look at one abnormal nail. Which preparatory step does the deck specify before inspection?","choices":["Leave the polish in place to avoid disturbing the nail plate, and inspect the periungual skin only","Inspect the affected nail under a Wood lamp before removing any polish","Remove the polish and inspect every nail, the periungual skin, the palms and soles, and the regional nodes","Remove the polish and inspect the affected nail alone, since comparison with other nails is unhelpful"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a single-nail problem is assessed against all the others. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 77-year-old has a recurrent basal cell carcinoma at the inner canthus, and the biopsy reports infiltrative histology. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Mohs micrographic surgery — the canthus is a high-risk site, the tumour is recurrent, and the histology is aggressive","Standard excision with 4 mm margins, since infiltrative histology behaves much like ordinary nodular disease","Topical imiquimod five nights weekly, since periocular skin tolerates surgical excision poorly","Hedgehog pathway inhibitor therapy with vismodegib, since recurrence at this site indicates advanced disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — three separate Mohs indications are met. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man's biopsy reports squamous cell carcinoma in situ on the forearm with no high-risk features. Which options does the deck give?","choices":["Programmed death 1 blockade or cetuximab only","Hedgehog pathway inhibitors, vismodegib or sonidegib","Imiquimod, topical fluorouracil","Surgical excision or Mohs micrographic surgery only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — three options for appropriately selected in situ disease. Or curettage and electrodesiccation. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 48-year-old has a red, eroded, mass-like lesion under one fingernail with no pigment at all. It has progressed over five months. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Treat empirically for onychomycosis and review in six months","Treat as a chronic paronychia with oral antibiotics and review in six weeks","Consider biopsy — amelanotic nail melanoma can look red, pink, eroded or mass-like","Reassure, because the complete absence of a pigmented band reliably excludes melanoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the absence of pigment does not exclude melanoma, so biopsy any unexplained, progressive single-nail lesion. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 52-year-old's melanoma is reported at 0.6 mm Breslow thickness with no adverse features. Which re-excision margin applies?","choices":["A 1 to 2 cm margin","A 3 cm margin","A 1 cm margin","A 0.5 to 1 cm margin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — lesions under 1 mm take a 1 cm margin. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A patient asks how likely his actinically induced squamous cell carcinoma is to spread. Which figure does the deck give, and what raises it?","choices":["An estimated 3 to 7%, raised substantially by high-risk sites or features, immunosuppression, multiple tumours, recurrence, or nodal involvement","An estimated 30 to 70%, raised further by high-risk sites or features, immunosuppression, multiple tumours, recurrence, or nodal involvement","An estimated 3 to 7%, and nothing raises it further, since metastatic risk is fixed at the moment of diagnosis whatever the site or immune status","Under 0.1%, raised only by nodal involvement already present at the time of diagnosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the baseline is modest but modifiable. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A patient treated for squamous cell carcinoma of the scalp returns with new numbness and tingling in the distribution of the scar. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Repeat topical fluorouracil to the scar and review in three months","Arrange routine review in twelve months as part of annual surveillance","Refer urgently, because neurologic symptoms are one of the deck's urgent referral triggers","Reassure, because sensory change is expected permanently after any scalp excision"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they raise concern for perineural invasion. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 64-year-old has a pigmented lesion on the nose with stippled pigment but also a pearly border, grown very slowly over four years. Which features help distinguish it from a melanocytic lesion?","choices":["The pearly border and the slow growth","The stippled pigmentation and the facial site","The absence of any surface change and the patient's age","The presence of ulceration and rapid growth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — pigmented basal cell carcinoma can mimic melanocytic disease. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 45-year-old patient has a suspicious pigmented lesion that needs sampling. Which is the initial diagnostic test the deck names?","choices":["Whole-body imaging before any tissue is taken","Biopsy or excision to confirm the diagnosis","Dermoscopy alone, with tissue reserved for lesions that later change","Sentinel lymph node biopsy as the first tissue step"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — tissue is required before staging decisions. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 74-year-old has had a scaly patch on his temple treated with cryotherapy twice over eight months, and it has come back again. It is now slightly firm to the touch. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat cryotherapy and review him again in a further three months","Start field therapy with topical fluorouracil across the whole temple","Reassure him that recurrence after cryotherapy is expected and that no further action is needed","Take a shave or punch biopsy, because persistence or recurrence after therapy is a biopsy trigger"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — recurrence after treatment, plus induration, is exactly what the deck says should prompt sampling. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has a melanoma in situ confirmed on his forearm. Which re-excision margin does the deck give?","choices":["A uniform 1 cm regardless of thickness","3 to 5 cm","0.5 to 1 cm","1 to 2 cm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — margins widen with Breslow thickness from there. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A patient asks why the pathologist's measurement of thickness matters so much. Which is the most accurate explanation?","choices":["Breslow thickness determines the cosmetic result of re-excision but has no bearing on prognosis","Breslow thickness is used only to decide the follow-up interval, not the margin or the staging","Breslow thickness matters only when nodes are already positive","Breslow thickness is the dominant prognostic variable and must be measured accurately at the initial biopsy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — which is why the initial biopsy technique matters. Ulceration and mitotic activity modifying the stage-based prognosis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has more than twenty rough scaly papules spread across her forehead, cheeks and nose. Individually they are small and none is indurated. Which treatment approach fits best?","choices":["Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy to each lesion in turn at this visit","Excision of each lesion with a two-millimetre margin","Observation alone, since none of the lesions is currently indurated or bleeding","Field-directed therapy such as topical fluorouracil, imiquimod or photodynamic therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — multiple lesions across one anatomic region is field cancerization, which is treated as a field. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A patient with a confirmed nail unit melanoma asks whether she will lose the finger. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Amputation is decided by the number of digits involved rather than by depth or bone involvement","Amputation is not automatic — digit-sparing wide excision or Mohs with immunostaining is contemporary care","Amputation is the automatic first treatment for any nail unit melanoma, whatever the depth or extent","Amputation is never performed for nail unit melanoma at any depth or extent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — amputation is reserved for deep, extensive or bone-involving disease, and the digit is preserved where margins can be reliably assessed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A patient treated for melanoma asks what she should be doing between appointments. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Monthly self-examination using the recognition mnemonic and ugly-duckling principles, including scalp, back, palms, soles and nails","Monthly self-examination of sun-exposed skin only, since melanoma does not arise on the scalp, back, palms, soles or nails","Annual self-examination using the recognition mnemonic and ugly-duckling principles, since more frequent checking causes anxiety","No self-examination of any site, since skin surveillance is entirely the specialist team's responsibility"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the sun-protected and acral sites are the ones people skip. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A patient with treated actinic keratoses asks whether he still needs follow-up now the lesions have gone. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Treatment eradicates the field as well as the lesions, so no further surveillance is needed","Lesions always involute spontaneously, so neither treatment nor surveillance was ever necessary","Surveillance is needed only if a lesion recurs at exactly the same site","Treatment reduces lesion burden but the surrounding field remains at risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — treating lesions does not treat the field. Ongoing surveillance is still necessary. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A patient with known Kaposi sarcoma has marked lower limb oedema but only three visible skin lesions. How should the oedema be interpreted?","choices":["Marked oedema indicates a correspondingly low disease burden, consistent with the small number of visible skin lesions","Marked oedema is not a feature of Kaposi sarcoma at any stage and points instead to venous thrombosis","Marked oedema indicates the classic rather than the epidemic form, and its severity gauges the disease burden","Marked oedema can occur even with few or no visible lesions, so its severity must not be used to gauge disease burden"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the skin can look almost clear while disease is extensive. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 64"}]};
